  "id": "10461",
  "self": "https://simeoncalixte1990.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/issue/10461",
  "key": "AIN-138",
  "fields": {
    "summary": "[AI settings] Configure organization models and individual inheritance",
    "description": {
      "type": "doc",
      "version": 1,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "As an organization administrator, I want to configure my organization\u2019s AI provider, model and credentials so that members and background processing can use our chosen model. As an individual, I want to inherit my current organization\u2019s configuration or use my own provider/model/credentials so that I control the model used for my work."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "User-approved scope expansion 2026-11-05: build self-service organization and individual settings, initially Gemini, Ollama, Qwen and Muse. Provider adapters are extensible; arbitrary model IDs supported by a configured adapter do not require per-model code. AIN-137 Muse support remains required and retains all original criteria. The user will help test their own account; do not infer their identity or copy personal credentials to organization scope."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Acceptance: authenticated Settings page shows effective source/provider/model and personal inheritance choice. Current organization administrators alone may change organization settings; active members may view redacted metadata and inherit. Any active individual, with or without an organization, may configure personal settings. Scope/actor derive from live identity membership, never caller-supplied tenant IDs; impersonation cannot change or test credentials. Cross-tenant reads/writes and revoked membership fail."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Acceptance: deterministic precedence is explicit personal override > current organization > existing platform configuration when no saved override exists. Choosing inherit removes personal override including its stored credential. Missing/invalid selected configuration fails visibly; no silent provider/model fallback. Organization automated work uses organization configuration; personal work uses the selected individual configuration. Effective provider/model provenance is recorded per execution; concurrent users cannot leak configuration via process.env."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Acceptance: existing providers plus Muse can be selected with configurable model IDs and relevant endpoint/timeout. Credentials are write-only, encrypted at rest with an operator-managed key, omitted from responses/logs/artifact snapshots, and replaceable/removable without returning existing values. Personal overrides never implicitly copy organization secrets. Protected endpoint policy prevents arbitrary user-controlled SSRF; operator-approved custom endpoints enable local Ollama and compatible services."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Acceptance: save/reload, inheritance/override/return-to-inheritance, organization member/admin permissions, explicit synthetic connection test, configuration validation, loading/empty/error/retry and revocation are implemented. Test action reports actual selected provider/model and failure without disclosing secrets. Do not run real document processing or change live credentials without the user choosing the action."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Verification: isolated synthetic Storybook transports block real writes and assert exact submitted payloads, observable results, permissions, keyboard/narrow layout and retry states; database/API integration tests prove durable encrypted state, current membership, authorization, concurrency isolation, and effective settings applied to pipeline consumers. Record ticket/component/story IDs/commands/results mapping. Configured provider and integrated staging evidence remain required; mocked stories cannot prove real Muse access. No pagination needed for a single settings record and a bounded provider list; model ID remains free text."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Dependencies: AIN-137 provides Muse adapter. Existing generated-form user outcome AIN-87 and release gate AIN-13 are related and retain broader obligations. Parent AIN-2 remains open until all original outcomes pass. Exclude pricing/capital financial model tickets, training/hosting new models, all other providers without an implemented adapter, and unrelated settings permissions repair. No sprint commitments or assignees invented."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
